 {
  "key": "algebra.polynomials.q14",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.algebra.polynomials"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 3,
  "estimatedSeconds": 270,
  "stem": "Prove that when a polynomial $f(x)$ is divided by $(ax + b)$, where $a \\neq 0$, the remainder is $f\\left(-\\dfrac{b}{a}\\right)$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 writes $f(x) \\equiv (ax + b)q(x) + R$ with $R$ a constant, because the divisor is linear",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 substitutes $x = -\\frac{b}{a}$, which makes $ax + b = 0$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 concludes $f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right) = 0 \\times q\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right) + R = R$",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "Dividing by a linear polynomial leaves a constant remainder $R$, so for all $x$\n$$\nf(x) \\equiv (ax + b)\\,q(x) + R.\n$$\nSince this is an identity it holds at $x = -\\frac{b}{a}$, where $ax + b = 0$:\n$$\nf\\left(-\\tfrac{b}{a}\\right) = 0 \\times q\\left(-\\tfrac{b}{a}\\right) + R = R.\n$$\nSo the remainder is $f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)$. (When $R = 0$, $(ax + b)$ is a factor: the factor theorem.)"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The proof rests on the division identity holding for every $x$, including the root of the divisor.",
  "hints": [
   "Start from the division statement f(x) ≡ (divisor)(quotient) + remainder."
  ],
  "skill": "UNDERSTANDING",
  "calculator": false
 },
